MOUNT_COMPLETENESS_RULE.value:
The p10-queue-card-foundations PAGE_ISOLATION gate asserted the INVERSE contract (no page may mount the queue primitives). It is retired - not deleted - and replaced by assert-mount-completeness.ps1, which verifies:
(1) each migrated page imports and renders QueueList;
(2) the retired table components no longer exist and nothing references them;
(3) no @tanstack/react-table import remains in ui/src AND the dependency is absent from BOTH ui/package.json and ui/package-lock.json;
(4) MOUNT_GATE_CLAUSE_4 - the per-page capability matrix, below;
(5) every id in PRESERVED_TESTIDS is still registered in test-ids.ts and every id in NEW_TESTIDS is registered there too;
(6) no legacy STATUS_COLORS map survives in ui/src;
(7) EMPTY_STATE_CONTRACT per page - the converter tree contains queue-empty-dropzone and queue-drop-strip, the downloader tree contains queue-empty-invite and NEITHER of the other two; and each of those ids satisfies MOUNT_GATE_CLAUSE_4 (c)+(d) in its own page's E2E spec, so a present-but-no-op Browse or invite button fails the clause;
(8) the E2E suite meets E2E_ASSERTION_CENSUS_RULE.

MOUNT_GATE_CLAUSE_4.value:
For EVERY (page, retired-id, HOME) triple in RETIRED_TOOLBAR_CONTROLS - iterating the per-page sub-maps and then every element of each id's home LIST, obligated per MOUNT_GATE_PHASE_SCOPES - the gate asserts all four of:
  (a) the retired id does not appear anywhere under ui/src (asserted once per retired id);
  (b) that home's testid appears in THAT page's rendered tree (the page component or a component it imports), not merely somewhere in ui/src;
  (c) at least one test in THAT page's E2E spec file locates that home and PERFORMS the interaction (click / keypress), not merely asserts visibility; and
  (d) that same test asserts an observable OUTCOME of the interaction - the intercepted request method+path+body, or the resulting DOM state - so a control that renders but is wired to nothing fails the gate.
(b)-(d) are evaluated PER HOME. A retired id with two homes yields two independent pass/fail results.
